---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"

const REPO = "https://github.com/cdrrazan/Docaru"
---
<Base
  title="Self-hosting — Docaru"
  description="One container and a MySQL database. Self-hosting is the stronger option and it is free — here is what it takes and what it costs you."
  path="/self-host"
>
  <h1>Run it yourself</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Self-hosting is the stronger option, and it is free. We would rather say that plainly than
    pretend our servers are safer than yours — the reason to choose this product is that nobody else
    can read your documents, and running it yourself is the most complete version of that.
  </p>

  <h2>What it takes</h2>

  <pre><code>{`git clone ${REPO}
cd Docaru
cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
docker compose up -d --build`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Two services: the application and a MySQL server. The application is a single process — the
    nightly reminder sweep runs inside it, so it cannot be the thing an operator forgets to start.
    The renewal rules are baked into the image, so an instance with no outbound network still knows
    every rule it shipped with.
  </p>

  <div class="panel panel-warn">
    <p class="eyebrow">Worth knowing before you rely on it</p>
    <p style="margin:0">
      The Docker image has never actually been built — this was developed without a Docker daemon
      available. The application itself runs against a real MySQL server and its test suite passes;
      the image around it is unexercised. If you are the first to try it, expect to fix something,
      and please open an issue when you do.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What you have to provide</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Thing</th><th>Needed for</th><th>Without it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>SMTP</td>
          <td>Reminder emails</td>
          <td>Nothing is delivered, which is the one job it has. Configure this first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A hostname</td>
          <td>Links inside those emails</td>
          <td>A relative link in an email goes nowhere.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>VAPID keys</td>
          <td>Push notifications</td>
          <td>Email still goes out; the toggle is hidden.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Paddle keys</td>
          <td>Billing</td>
          <td>Every household stays free. Nothing is paywalled anyway.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Backups</td>
          <td>Your documents surviving your disk</td>
          <td>Nobody else has a copy. That is the point, and it is also the risk.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Back up both volumes</h2>

  <p>
    <code>docaru-db</code> holds the records and the deadlines. <code>docaru-storage</code> holds the
    encrypted scans. Neither is useful without the other, and neither is useful without your vault
    passphrase — which is not in either of them, because it never left your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>What you give up</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing in the software. Reminders, playbooks, the vault, the passphrase recovery and the
    prerequisite chains all work identically; there is no feature held back for the hosted version
    and no phone-home. What you take on is the operations: upgrades, TLS, and backups that you have
    actually tested restoring.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO}#self-hosting`}>Full instructions are in the README</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
